  <h2>TL;DR</h2>
  <p class="answer-first">
    A useful Done-For-You engagement is not a promise that the practice can stop participating. ChiroCandy can own the marketing execution named in the agreement; the practice owns the facts, approvals, access, capacity, inquiry handoff, patient communication, and business outcomes needed to make that work usable.
  </p>

  <h2>What should a Done-For-You proposal define?</h2>
  <p>
    The proposal should be a scope sheet, not a list of possible marketing channels. It should name the locations, services, audiences, deliverables, cadence, media budget, usage charges, exclusions, optional work, review points, and term that apply to your practice.
  </p>
  <p>For each included service, confirm:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The work:</strong> strategy, setup, creative, pages, publishing, campaign management, follow-up, reporting, or another explicitly named deliverable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The destination:</strong> the approved website, landing page, phone route, form, inbox, CRM, or calendar used by that work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The owner:</strong> who creates, approves, publishes, monitors, responds, and resolves exceptions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The evidence:</strong> what will be reported, the definition of each stage, the source system, and what remains unavailable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The boundary:</strong> work not included, practice responsibilities, third-party costs, and the change process when scope expands.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    ChiroCandy’s <a href="/services/">services hub</a> and individual service pages explain the available categories. Your current proposal and <a href="/pricing/">written terms</a> control the actual engagement; a service appearing on the website does not make it part of every package.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep an asset and access register</h2>
  <p>
    Before work begins, list every domain, website, landing-page account, advertising account, Business Profile, analytics property, tag manager, CRM, calendar, phone or messaging number, creative library, and reporting dashboard needed for the agreed scope.
  </p>
  <p>For each asset, record:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the practice administrator, agency user, billing owner, and approver;</li>
    <li>the access level required for the assignment and who can remove it;</li>
    <li>whether the asset is practice-owned, agency-owned, or licensed under the agreement;</li>
    <li>the export, transition, and offboarding path; and</li>
    <li>which live workflow would stop if access changed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Keep passwords and recovery codes out of the register. Durable practice access and individual user roles are easier to audit than a shared login. The <a href="/blog/essential-chiropractic-marketing-tools/">chiropractic marketing tools audit</a> and <a href="/blog/chiropractic-domain-website-ownership/">website ownership checklist</a> provide deeper worksheets.
  </p>

  <h2>What does the practice still need to own?</h2>
  <p>
    Delegating execution does not delegate the practice’s responsibility for accurate services, locations, clinician information, approved offers, clinical review, patient communication, calendar capacity, and staff decisions. Name a practice owner for approvals and an operational owner for inquiry handoff.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Facts and approvals:</strong> supply current business information, approve claims and creative, and flag changes before they make live work inaccurate.</li>
    <li><strong>Capacity and fit:</strong> state which locations and services can accept demand and when the plan should pause or change.</li>
    <li><strong>Inquiry response:</strong> define who handles new contacts, absences, duplicates, existing patients, urgent messages, and contacts that are not a fit.</li>
    <li><strong>Appointment records:</strong> keep the official calendar and the definitions for booked, cancelled, rescheduled, and held appointments.</li>
    <li><strong>Patient and financial outcomes:</strong> use approved practice systems for patient status and collected revenue rather than inferring either from a marketing dashboard.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use a launch-readiness gate</h2>
  <p>A channel is ready to launch when the agreed prerequisites have evidence, not merely when a campaign switch can be turned on. Confirm that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>scope, locations, audience, offer, budget, destinations, and exclusions are approved;</li>
    <li>required account access works and the practice retains the agreed administrative control;</li>
    <li>creative and page facts match the approved services and location;</li>
    <li>forms, calls, messages, and routing have been tested with non-sensitive data;</li>
    <li>the responder, backup, calendar owner, and exception path are named;</li>
    <li>baseline definitions and reporting sources are documented; and</li>
    <li>known holds—such as missing access, approval, capacity, or integration support—are visible rather than bypassed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The launch date should come from the current work plan because access, approvals, production, integrations, and practice response readiness vary. A universal timeline would hide those dependencies.
  </p>

  <h2>Separate marketing activity from practice outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    A useful report keeps delivery, platform results, relevant inquiries, appointment requests, booked appointments, held appointments, patients, and collected revenue separate. Each stage needs its own definition, denominator, source, date range, and owner. Missing downstream data should remain unavailable rather than becoming zero or an estimated success.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google Analytics likewise documents separate recommended events for generating, qualifying, working, and closing leads. Those events require implementation and context; an event name is not proof that an appointment occurred. See <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/9267735?hl=en">Google Analytics recommended events</a> and the more detailed <a href="/blog/chiropractic-advertising-leads-to-appointments/">lead-to-appointment handoff guide</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Search and AI visibility require the same restraint. Google says ordinary search eligibility and helpful content apply to AI features, and meeting its requirements does not guarantee crawling, indexing, or serving. A proposal can define the work ChiroCandy will perform; it cannot truthfully guarantee a map position or AI citation. See <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features">Google’s AI features guidance</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is a good fit for Done-For-You?</h2>
  <p>
    Done-For-You may fit an established chiropractic practice that wants outside execution, has appointment capacity, can provide timely approvals and access, assigns an owner to inquiry handling, and is willing to review evidence without treating every marketing result as a patient outcome.
  </p>
  <p>
    It may be premature when no one can approve the work, account ownership is disputed, the office cannot support more demand, the destination or handoff is broken, or the practice expects a guaranteed ranking, citation, appointment count, or revenue result. In that case, the first useful project is the readiness gap—not a larger channel list.
  </p>

  <h2>How is this different from the Training Program?</h2>
  <p class="answer-first">
    Done-For-You assigns agreed marketing execution to ChiroCandy. The <a href="/training-program/">Training Program</a> is for practices that want their own team to learn and execute the playbooks. <a href="/get-new-patients/">Get New Patients</a> explains the acquisition-system question; this page explains the managed-engagement boundary.
  </p>
  <p>
    Compare the options by execution capacity, required access, approval speed, staff handoff, evidence needs, and the work actually written into the proposal. Do not choose a program based on a promise that the practice will have no responsibilities.
  </p>

  <h2>Bring the operating questions to the discovery call</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/schedule/">Schedule a Marketing &amp; AI Discovery Call</a> and bring your current accounts, website and CRM ownership, appointment capacity, inquiry-response workflow, reporting definitions, and the proposal terms you need clarified. The useful next step is a documented fit and readiness decision—not a promised outcome.
  </p>
